<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sino-Burmese War (1765–1769)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a>–<a href="/years/1769">1769</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Qing invasion of Burma · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sino-Burmese War was fought from 1765 to 1769 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> and the Konbaung dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> sent four invasions into Burma, all of which were repulsed, the last ending with the Treaty of Kaungton (1769); Burma kept its independence, but the war diverted it from its campaigns against Siam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sino-burmese-war-1765-1769.jpg" alt="Sino-Burmese War (1765–1769)" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: w:Soewinhan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qing_and_Konbaung_before_Sino_Burmese_War.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The long border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> had never been demarcated, and the Shan sawbwas, or chiefs, of the borderlands paid tribute to both sides. In the 1730s the Qing tightened their hold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>'s frontier, crushing Shan revolts and winning over neutral sawbwas with titles, so that by 1735 ten had sided with them. After the Konbaung dynasty reunited Burma, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a> sent an expedition in 1758–59 to restore Burmese authority in the farther <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan States</a>, and the chiefs of Mogaung, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhamo">Bhamo</a> and Hsenwi fled into Yunnan and urged the Qing to invade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>With the main Burmese army away invading Siam, the governor Liu Zao used minor trade disputes as a pretext to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kengtung">Kengtung</a> in December 1765 with 3,500 Green Standard troops, who were defeated and pursued into Pu'er. In 1766 Yang Yingju struck at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhamo">Bhamo</a>, but his army was surrounded near the Burmese fort at Kaungton and wasted by cholera, dysentery and malaria. After he sent false reports of victory, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> ordered him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, where he killed himself on the emperor's order.</p><p>The emperor then sent Manchu Bannermen under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-rui">Ming Rui</a>, whose army came within a few days' march of Ava before tropical terrain and disease turned it back, and it was destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maymyo">Battle of Maymyo</a> in 1768. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hsinbyushin">Hsinbyushin</a>, who had refused to recall his armies from Siam until then, brought them to the Chinese front. In 1769 Fuheng led 60,000 men down the Irrawaddy with war boats built by his own carpenters, but was encircled near Kaungton. Having lost 20,000 men, the Chinese command asked for terms, and Maha Thiha Thura agreed to a truce rather than provoke yet another invasion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war cost the lives of over 70,000 Qing soldiers and four commanders. The emperor rejected the treaty, kept heavy forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> for about a decade and banned cross-border trade for two; when Burmese missions resumed in 1788 the Qing treated them as submission and claimed victory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> kept the nine states above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhamo">Bhamo</a>, and its defence laid the foundation of the present border. The chief beneficiary was Siam, which by 1770 had won back most of its lost territory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sino-Burmese War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Burmese did not stay to rule the country, partly because a <b>Qing invasion of Burma</b> demanded their armies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
